It's often heard that if there's not anything new to a suggestion there
will be some reluctance to consider adding that. Let me ask some questions;
a) Is there already any trap effect in the game in case you drop items
    onto the trap or would that need to be specifically implemented?
b) Water is already a widely available general purpose diluting mechanism
    for books, scrolls, and most potions; do we need another one for books?
c) Would any implementation of a new interaction of "single type of trap"
    with a "single type of object" make it necessary to extend it for all
    (or most) traps and for all (or most) objects, just to make that feature
    consistent?

> Why have credit-cards when there are also lock-picks in the game?

Credit cards just open locks; that's a difference for one who found no key
or lock-pick in case he intends to use locked doors as a tactical means.
(Though, I am less certain in case of keys and lock-picks; are there any
differences?)

> It will just add more detail and contribute to the TDTTOE thing. 

Not sure about that; see my point c) above.

So I'd say; it's no bad suggestion, but I also second the opinion you've
been responding to that it's not worthwhile.

